# Chapter 1, part 3: command keys, the F12 menu, accelerators, markers.
.h2 The keys [view-keys]
.ix Keys
.ix Keyboard
.ix Command keys
.ix Function keys
.ix Keystroke summary
Strata is driven from the keyboard.  A command is given in one of four ways:
by a function key ({n:view-fkeys}), by an accelerator, which is a Ctrl letter
({n:view-accel}), by a choice from the {k:F12} menu ({n:view-menu}), or by a
marker inserted with the Marker command ({n:view-markers}).  Most commands
can be reached in more than one way.  This section lists every key that the
Editor reads, in groups.  In the tables the column Mode says S for a key that
works only in a structured document, U for a key that works only in an
unstructured one and B for both.

.h3 Typing text [view-typing]
.ix Typing
.ix Backspace
.ix Del
.ix Tab key
.ix Enter key
.ix Overtype
.ix Alt-O
.ix OVR
.table 15 39 5
Key | Effect | Mode
Printing key | Insert the character at the cursor.  In Overtype it | B
 | replaces the character under the cursor. | 
{k:Enter} | End the line.  The lines stay in the same block. | B
{k:Tab} | Insert a tab.  The text after it begins at the next tab stop. | B
{k:Backspace} | Delete the character before the cursor.  At the start of a | B
 | block, join the block to the one before it (both of one column). | 
{k:Del} | Delete the character at the cursor.  At the end of a block, | B
 | join the next block to it; on an empty block, delete the block. | 
{k:Ctrl-D} | Delete the next word. | B
{k:Alt-Backspace} | Delete the previous word. | B
{k:Alt-F5}, {k:Alt-O} | Overtype on or off.  The message line shows | B
 | {c:Overtype on} or {c:Overtype off}; {c:OVR} is on the status line. |
{k:Ctrl-Shift-Space} | Insert a no-break space ({n:edit-nbsp}). | B
{k:Alt-_} | Insert a soft hyphen ({n:appchar}). | B
.end

.note {k:Alt-Backspace} is the key that deletes the previous word.
  {k:Ctrl-Backspace} is the same as {k:Backspace} in the X11 version (the
  Ctrl is ignored) and is {k:Ctrl-H}, Hanging indent, in a terminal.

.h3 Moving the cursor [view-moving]
.ix Cursor movement
.ix Moving around the document
.ix Go To
.ix Word, moving by
.table 15 39 5
Key | Effect | Mode
{k:Left}, {k:Right} | One character left, right.  At the end of a line the | B
 | cursor goes on to the next. | 
{k:Up}, {k:Down} | One line up, down. | B
{k:PgUp}, {k:PgDn} | About half a screen up, down. | B
{k:Home}, {k:End} | The beginning, the end of the line. | B
{k:Ctrl-Left} | The start of the word to the left. | B
{k:Ctrl-Right} | The start of the next word. | B
{k:Ctrl-Home} | The beginning of the block (of its column). | B
{k:Ctrl-End} | The end of the block (of its column). | B
{k:Shift-Home} | The beginning of the document. | B
{k:Shift-End} | The end of the document. | B
{k:Shift-Left} | The column to the left in the same block; in the first | B
 | column, the headline of the enclosing section (one level up). | 
{k:Shift-Right} | The next column of the same block; in the last column, | B
 | the headline of the first subsection (one level down). | 
{k:Shift-Up} | The headline of the current section; when the cursor is | S
 | on it already, the headline of the previous section. | 
{k:Shift-Down} | The headline of the next section, of any level. | S
{k:Shift-PgUp} | The headline of the previous section of the same level, | S
 | not crossing a section of a higher level. | 
{k:Shift-PgDn} | The headline of the next section of the same level. | S
{k:Alt-F7} | Go To: a menu (below). | B
.end

Go To ({k:Alt-F7}, or {k:Ctrl-F} then Go To) opens a list of five choices:
Document start, Document end, Section number..., Next section and Previous
section.  The fourth and the fifth do what {k:Shift-Down} and {k:Shift-Up}
do.  Section number... prompts for a number such as {c:2.1} and puts the cursor
in the headline of that section; if there is no such section the message
{c:No section 2.1} is shown.

The keys that go by headlines work on the sections that are shown: a section
that is hidden by Edit, Section is not reached, and one that is collapsed
shows only its headline.

.h3 The outline: showing and hiding [view-outline-keys]
.ix Outline
.ix Collapse
.ix Expand
.ix Alt-1 to Alt-9
.ix Alt-0
.ix Alt-+
.ix Alt--
.ix Alt-*
.ix Keypad +
.ix Keypad -
.ix Keypad *
.ix Fold state
.ix Section tag, + and *
In a structured document the text and the subsections of a section can be
folded out of sight, which shows the outline of the document.  The state of
the folds is part of the file: changing it modifies the document, and Quit
offers to save it.

.table 18 36 5
Key | Effect | Mode
{k:Keypad -}, {k:Alt--} | Collapse the current section to its headline. | S
{k:Keypad +}, {k:Alt-+}, {k:Alt-=} | Expand the current section one step: first | S
 | show its text, then its subsections as headlines. |
{k:Keypad *}, {k:Alt-*} | Show or hide the text of the section's blocks | S
 | (the headline stays). | 
{k:Alt-1} to {k:Alt-9} | Show the headlines down to that level.  Pressed | S
 | again, show them with their text. | 
{k:Alt-0} | Outline the whole document: only headlines; again, all. | S
.end

On the screen a section that is folded has a mark in its double line:
a {c:*} when the text of the section is hidden and a {c:+} when the
subsections are hidden as well.  The picture shows the same report after
{k:Alt-2}: the sections of level 1 are headlines with a star, and the
sections of level 2 have a plus because their subsections are hidden.

.pic pictures/view-outline.png 5.4 The outline at level 2.

In an unstructured document these keys show the message {c:Collapse/expand
works in structured documents}.  The keypad keys: see the note on
{k:Keypad +} in {x:view-form-keys}.

.h3 Sections, blocks and columns [view-sbc]
.ix Append
.ix Split
.ix Join
.ix Cut block
.ix Cut section
.ix Promote section
.ix Demote section
.ix Alt-[
.ix Alt-]
.ix Ins (append section)
.table 15 39 5
Key | Effect | Mode
{k:Ins} | Append a section of the same level, after the current one | B
 | and all its subsections.  In an unstructured document it appends a | 
 | block that has the global format. | 
{k:Shift-Ins} | Append a subsection: the last subsection of the current | B
 | section.  In an unstructured document, the same as Ins. | 
{k:Ctrl-A} | Append a block with the same local format as the current. | B
{k:Ctrl-O} | Append a block with the global format. | B
{k:Alt-F2} | Append: a menu of Column, Block, Global-block, | B
 | Peer-section and Sub-section (the last two in a structured document). | 
{k:F2} | Split: a menu of Column, Block, Section. | B
{k:Ctrl-V} | Split the block at the cursor. | B
{k:Ctrl-Y} | Split the section at the cursor. | S
{k:Alt-F3} | Join: a menu of Block, Section. | B
{k:Ctrl-K} | Cut the block that holds the cursor. | B
{k:Ctrl-L} | Cut the section that holds the cursor. | S
{k:Alt-F4} | Delete: the unit menu, then highlight. | B
{k:Alt-[} {k:Alt-<} | Promote the section one level (it becomes a | S
 | peer of its parent, placed after it).  {k:Alt-,} also. | 
{k:Alt-]} {k:Alt->} | Demote the section one level (it becomes the | S
 | last subsection of the section before it).  {k:Alt-.} also. | 
{k:Ctrl-^} | Edit Section; then Edit Document. | S
{k:F8} | Table: a menu of Add column and Split column here. | B
.end

In a terminal {k:Alt-[} cannot be told from the start of a key sequence;
{k:Alt-<} and {k:Alt->} (or {k:Alt-,} and {k:Alt-.}) are the keys that
work there.  The subsections of a section go with it when it moves.  With the
sections 2, 2.1, 2.2 and 2.3, promoting 2.1 gives 3 with the sections 3.1
and 3.2 (the old 2.2 and 2.3) below it.

.h3 Highlight, copy, cut, paste [view-copykeys]
.ix Copy
.ix Cut
.ix Paste
.ix Move
.ix Library
.ix Lib Copy
.ix Lib Cut
.ix Lib Paste
.table 9 45 5
Key | Effect | Mode
{k:F3} | Copy: the unit menu, then highlight, then Enter. | B
{k:F4} | Cut: the same, and the area is removed. | B
{k:F5} | Paste what the last Copy or Cut kept, at the cursor. | B
{k:F7} | Move: move columns, blocks or sections in the place. | B
{k:Ctrl-X} | Cut text: highlight text at once (no unit menu), Enter to | B
 | cut. | 
{k:Ctrl-F3} | Lib Copy: copy to the library (the unit menu first). | B
{k:Ctrl-F4} | Lib Cut: cut to the library. | B
{k:Ctrl-F5} | Lib Paste: paste an entry of the library. | B
{k:Enter} | In highlight mode: accept. | B
{k:Esc} | In highlight mode: cancel. | B
.end

.h3 Attributes and formats [view-attrkeys]
.ix Bold
.ix Italics
.ix Underscore
.ix Normal text
.ix Set Attributes
.ix Ctrl-B
.ix Ctrl-U
.ix Ctrl-N
.ix Alt-I
.table 15 39 5
Key | Effect | Mode
{k:Ctrl-B} | Bold on or off. | B
{k:Alt-I} | Italics on or off ({k:Ctrl-I} is the Tab key). | B
{k:Ctrl-U} | Underscore on or off. | B
{k:Ctrl-N} | Normal text: all attributes off. | B
{k:Alt-F6} | Set Attributes: a menu of Normal, Bold, Italics and | B
 | Underscore Words. | 
{k:F6}, {k:Ctrl-E} | The Block Format Editor for the block and column. | B
{k:Ctrl-H} | Hanging indent: set the tab and the indent so that the lines | B
 | after the first start where the text now starts. | 
.end

An attribute key puts a switch into the text at the cursor, a mark that
changes the attribute from there on: the text typed next, and the text that
already follows up to the next attribute mark, has the new attribute.  The
same key at the end of the stretch turns the attribute off.  The marks are
not shown in the text; the field Attr of the status line shows the attribute
at the cursor.  {n:edit} gives the exact behavior.

.h3 Undo and Redo [view-undo-keys]
.ix Undo
.ix Redo
.ix Ctrl-/
.ix Ctrl-F10
.table 24 30 5
Key | Effect | Mode
{k:Ctrl-/}, {k:Ctrl-_}, {k:Ctrl-Z}, {k:Ctrl-F10} | Undo the last change. | B
{k:Alt-/}, {k:Ctrl-F11} | Redo what was undone. | B
.end
{n:edit-b-undo} describes both and the number of levels.

.h3 Search, replace, markers, files [view-miscellaneous]
.ix Search
.ix Replace
.ix Marker key
.ix Save key
.ix Retrieve key
.ix Quit key
.table 15 39 5
Key | Effect | Mode
{k:F9} | Search: prompts for a string and goes to the next place. | B
{k:Alt-F9} | Replace: the panel; prompts at each place, or | B
 | changes all of them at once. |
{k:Alt-F12}, {k:Ctrl-F12} | Marker: the Marker Commands grid, or the panel | B
 | of the marker under the cursor. |
{k:Alt-F8} | Border: the Border panel ({n:edit-c-border}). | B
{k:Ctrl-F2} | Math: calculate in a column or a table ({n:edit-c-math}). | B
{k:Ctrl-F6} | Case: change the case of the highlighted text | B
 | ({n:edit-c-case}). |
{k:Ctrl-F9} | Again: search for the string of the last Search. | B
{k:Ctrl-F7}, {k:Ctrl-F8} | Go to the previous, the next print error | B
 | ({n:print-errors}). |
{k:Ctrl-G} | Text indent: insert the text indent character. | B
{k:Ctrl-S} | Save the document at once, under its own name. | B
{k:Ctrl-R} | Retrieve: the panel of File, Retrieve. | B
{k:Ctrl-Q} | Quit the Editor.  A changed document puts a question first. | B
{k:Ctrl-P} | Print, Final. | B
{k:F12} | The menu bar. | B
{k:F1} | Help for the edit screen (anywhere in the Editor). | B
{k:Ctrl-F} | The menu of the Alt commands (below). | B
{k:Ctrl-W} | In the equation editor only: show the typeset equation. | B
{k:Ctrl-C} | Spell check of the word at the cursor ({n:spell}). | B
{k:Ctrl-T} | Thesaurus for the word at the cursor ({n:spell}). | B
{k:Alt-A} to {k:Alt-Z} | Run the macro of that key, if there is one. | B
{k:Ctrl-F1} | Learn: start the recording of a macro, and end it (see Tools, Macros, Learn). | B
.end

.caution A macro takes its key before any other command does.  If you give a
  macro a key that is also a command key ({k:Alt-O}, {k:Alt-I}, {k:Alt-1}),
  the macro runs and the command is lost for as long as the macro exists.

.h3 The Ctrl-F menu [view-ctrlf]
.ix Ctrl-F
.ix Alt commands menu
.ix Commands menu, function keys
.ix Function keys, list of commands
Many terminals and window managers keep the function keys with Alt or Ctrl for
themselves.  {k:Ctrl-F} opens a list headed {c:Commands} with every command
that has a function key, each with its key: {c:Copy (F3)}, {c:Search Again
(Ctrl-F9)}, {c:Border (Alt-F8)} and the others.  Choose with the cursor keys
and {k:Enter}, which runs the command as the key does; {k:Esc} leaves.  The
list is longer than the screen and scrolls.  It ends with three commands
whose keys a terminal may not pass either: {c:Promote section (Alt-[)},
{c:Demote section (Alt-])} and {c:Soft hyphen (Alt-_)}.  The list is made from
the same table as the keys, so a command is in it when it has a key and in no
other case.  A key that has a label on the key strip but no command, such as
{k:Alt-F1}, is not in it.

.pic pictures/view-altmenu.png 5.4 The Commands list that {k:Ctrl-F} opens (X11 version): it scrolls.

.h3 Alternative keys for terminals [view-terminal-keys]
.ix Terminal keys, alternates
.ix Alternates for terminals
The keys that a terminal may not pass on have alternates that work in both
versions: {k:Ctrl-F} for the Alt function keys, {k:Ctrl-F12} for
{k:Alt-F12}, {k:Alt-O} for {k:Alt-F5}, {k:Alt-I} for {k:Ctrl-I},
{k:Alt-<} and {k:Alt->} for
{k:Alt-[} and {k:Alt-]}, {k:Alt-+}, {k:Alt--} and {k:Alt-*} for the keypad
keys, and {k:F2} for {k:Keypad +} in a panel.  {n:appterm} lists them.

.h2 The function keys [view-fkeys]
.ix Function keys
.ix F1 to F12
.ix Alt-F keys
.ix Ctrl-F keys
.ix Shift-F keys
The function keys have three layers, as the labels on the screen show.  The
plain keys are the commands used most.  The Alt layer holds the commands that
append, join and delete, and the Border, Go To and Marker commands.  The Ctrl
layer holds the library, the macro recording, Math, Case, the print errors
and Undo and Redo.

.table 8 15 36
Key | Command | What it does
{k:F1} | Help | Opens the help viewer on the topic Edit Screen.
{k:F2} | Split | Split Column, Block or Section at the cursor.
{k:F3} | Copy | Copy text, a column, blocks or sections.
{k:F4} | Cut | Cut them.
{k:F5} | Paste | Insert what was copied or cut.
{k:F6} | Format | The Block Format Editor.
{k:F7} | Move | Move columns, blocks or sections in the place.
{k:F8} | Table | Add column, Split column here.
{k:F9} | Search | Search for a string.
{k:F12} | Menu | The menu bar.
{k:Alt-F2} | Append | Column, Block, Global-block, Peer-section,
 | | Sub-section.
{k:Alt-F3} | Join | Block, Section.
{k:Alt-F4} | Delete | The unit menu, then highlight, then Enter.
{k:Alt-F5} | Overtype | On or off.
{k:Alt-F6} | Set Attribute | Normal, Bold, Italics, Underscore Words.
{k:Alt-F7} | Go To | The Go To list.
{k:Alt-F8} | Border | The Border panel.
{k:Alt-F9} | Replace | Search and replace: prompts at each place.
{k:Alt-F12} | Marker | Marker Commands, or edit the marker under the cursor.
{k:Ctrl-F1} | Learn | Start recording a macro; end it.
{k:Ctrl-F2} | Math | Calculate in a column or a table.
{k:Ctrl-F3} | Lib Copy | Copy to the library.
{k:Ctrl-F4} | Lib Cut | Cut to the library.
{k:Ctrl-F5} | Lib Paste | Paste from the library.
{k:Ctrl-F6} | Case | Change the case of the highlighted text.
{k:Ctrl-F7} | Prev Error | Go to the previous print error.
{k:Ctrl-F8} | Next Error | Go to the next print error.
{k:Ctrl-F9} | Again | Search for the same string again.
{k:Ctrl-F10} | Undo | Undo the last change.
{k:Ctrl-F11} | Redo | Redo what was undone.
{k:Ctrl-F12} | Marker | The same as Alt-F12, for terminals.
.end
{k:F10} and {k:F11} have no command, and a macro keeps them and plays them
back.  {k:Alt-F1}, {k:Alt-F10} and {k:Alt-F11} show the message
{c:Alt-F1: not available} (with their own number).  {k:Shift} with {k:F1} to
{k:F12} does nothing.  {k:F12} and its forms belong to the menu bar and to the
Marker command: a macro never keeps them.

.h2 The F12 menu [view-menu]
.ix Menu bar
.ix F12 menu
.ix Command menu
.ix Menu, F12
.ix MENU (label)
{k:F12} opens the menu bar on the two top rows of the screen, over the
status line.  The first row has the names of the entries, with the one
that is chosen in reverse video; the second row says in one line what that
entry does.  At the right end of the first row a label names the path:
{c:MENU}, {c:MENU-GLOBAL}, {c:MENU-TOOLS-MACROS} and so on.

.pic pictures/view-menu-top.png 5.4 The menu bar (F12).

An entry that opens another list replaces the names on the first row with
those of its entries.  The top level is

.code
File  Global  Edit  Print  Tools  Quit
.end

.table 13 47
Key | Effect in the menu
{k:Left}, {k:Right} | Choose the entry to the left, to the right.  They wrap
 | round at the ends.
{k:Home}, {k:End} | The first, the last entry.
{k:Enter}, {k:Ins}, {k:Down} | Open the entry (a list below it) or carry the
 | command out.
A letter | The entry whose name begins with that letter, and open it.
{k:Esc} | Go up one level; at the top level, close the menu.
{k:F12} | Close the menu from any level.
.end

The Editor draws the menu over the document and removes it when a command is
chosen.

.pic pictures/view-menu-global.png 5.4 The menu bar one level down: Global.

