{k:Ctrl-C} | Spell check of the current word |
{k:Ctrl-D} | Delete the next word |
{k:Ctrl-E} | Block Format Editor | {k:F6}
{k:Ctrl-F} | The menu of Alt commands |
{k:Ctrl-H} | Hanging indent | ({k:F6})
{k:Ctrl-I} | A tab | Tab
{k:Ctrl-K} | Cut the block | {k:F4}, Block
{k:Ctrl-L} | Cut the section | {k:F4}, Section
{k:Ctrl-N} | Normal text | Alt-F6, Normal
{k:Ctrl-O} | Append a block, global format | Alt-F2, Global
{k:Ctrl-P} | Print, Final | Print, Final
{k:Ctrl-Q} | Quit | {k:F12}, Quit
{k:Ctrl-R} | Retrieve | File, Retrieve
{k:Ctrl-S} | Save | File, Save
{k:Ctrl-T} | Thesaurus for the current word |
{k:Ctrl-U} | Underscore on or off | Alt-F6
{k:Ctrl-V} | Split the block at the cursor | {k:F2}, Block
{k:Ctrl-W} | View the equation (equation editor) |
{k:Ctrl-X} | Cut text | {k:F4}, Text
{k:Ctrl-Y} | Split the section at the cursor | {k:F2}, Section
{k:Ctrl-^} | Edit Section or Document | Edit menu
.end

.note {k:Ctrl-J} and {k:Ctrl-M} end the line as {k:Enter} does, and {k:Ctrl-I}
  is {k:Tab}.  A terminal sends the same code for the Ctrl form of those letters and
  for the keys themselves.

.h2 Markers in brief [view-markers]
.ix Markers
.ix Marker commands
.ix Backslash commands
.ix Marker menu
.ix Insert a marker
.ix Edit a marker
A marker is a command in the text.  A character stands for itself; a marker
stands for a value that the Editor or the Printer works out: today's date, a
page number, a footnote, a picture, an equation, a line of a given length, a
cross-reference to another section.  A marker is one object in the line of
text and moves with the text when the text is moved, copied or cut.  A marker
is chosen from a list and its panel is filled in.

.h3 Inserting and changing a marker [view-marker-use]
.ix Marker, insert
.ix Marker, change
.ix Marker panel
.ix Invalid marker selection
Press {k:Alt-F12} (or {k:Ctrl-F12}, or {k:Ctrl-F} and then Marker) with the
cursor where the marker is to go.  The Marker Commands grid appears
({n:view-grid}).  Choose a name with the cursor keys or by its
first letter and press {k:Ins}.  A marker that has fields opens its panel
(for a Date marker, the Format of the date); fill it in and press {k:Ins}.  A
marker without fields is inserted at once.  The marker takes the place of
the cursor, and the document is changed.  {k:Esc} in the grid or in the
panel inserts nothing.

To change a marker that is already in the text, put the cursor on it and press
{k:Alt-F12}: the panel of that marker opens at once, with its values.
{k:Esc} leaves it as it was.

A name that is in parentheses cannot be used where the cursor is.  Choosing
it shows the box {c:Marker} with the text {c:Invalid marker selection (Not
available in an unstructured document)} or {c:Invalid marker selection (Not
available in document body)}.

.h3 The markers [view-marker-list]
.ix Marker list
.ix Marker names
Strata has 35 markers.  The Marker Commands grid lists them in this order.

.table 15 6 37
Marker | Where | What it is for
Author | B | The author of the document, from its header.
Column Break | B | Begins the text that follows in the next column.
Comment | B | A note to yourself that is not printed.  It has blocks.
Counter | B | A number that counts up, with a style; there are 99 counters.
Cross-reference | S | The number, the headline or the page of a section.
Date | B | The date, in a format that you choose.
Description | B | The description of the document, from its header.
Else | B | The other branch of an If.
Endif | B | The end of an If.
Equation | B | An equation, shown as text or, in X11, as a picture.
Figure | B | A caption number for the table of figures.
Filedate | B | The date of the file.
Filename | B | The name of the file.
Filetime | B | The time of the file.
Font | B | A change of font from here on.
If | B | Text that is used only if an expression is true.
Include | B | The text of another document, put here at print.
Index | B | An entry of the index: heading, subheading, text.
Keep | B | Keep what follows together on one page.
Keycap | B | A key drawn as a key: {c:Legend}, shades, scale, font.
LaTeX | B | LaTeX instructions, handed to LaTeX as they are typed.
Line | B | A line, of a length and a weight, or a string repeated.
Merge | B | A field that print merge fills in.
Note | B | A footnote, a levelnote or an endnote.  It has blocks.
Orientation | B | Portrait or landscape page.
Page Break | B | Start the next text on a new, an odd or an even page.
Page Number | H | The number of the page.  Headers and footers only.
Pathname | B | The directory and name of the file.
Picture | B | A picture from a file: PNG, JPEG, PDF or EPS.
Printer | B | The name of the printer.
Revision | B | The revision of the document, from its header.
Section | H | The number of the section.  Headers and footers only.
Table | B | A caption number for the table of tables.
Time | B | The time, in a format that you choose.
Whitespace | B | A vertical space of a size.
.end
In the column Where, B means the marker can be used anywhere, S only in
a structured document and H only in a header or a footer (in the body Page
Number and Section are in parentheses).  The markers are the subject of
{n:markers}, where every marker has its panel, its fields and its effect
described.

.h3 How a marker looks on the screen [view-marker-look]
.ix Marker, display modes
.ix Marker Display Modes
.ix Diamond (marker)
Each marker is drawn in the text according to the setting Marker Display
Modes of the Global, Marker panel, which has a mode for each kind of marker:
the value (the date itself), its short label ({c:[WS 0.3 in]}), a long
label, a diamond, its name, or nothing at all.  Markers are drawn in cyan
(the terminal) or in the color of the markers (X11).  In the X11 version
equations, keycaps, pictures and LaTeX markers are drawn as pictures when
their mode is Inline.  While the cursor is on a marker the field Attr of the
status line shows {c:Special}.  A marker is one unit for the editing keys:
{k:Del} removes it, and the cursor moves over it in one step.
